            <ArticleTitle>Safety of immediate treadmill testing in selected emergency department patients with chest pain: a preliminary report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>557-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>To determine the feasibility and safety of an immediate, symptom-limited, treadmill test on selected emergency department (ED) patients, a convenience sample of 28 patients underwent an exercise treadmill test (ETT) within the first several hours after hospital arrival using the modified Bruce protocol. Patients were included in the study if they presented with otherwise unexplained chest pain consistent with (but not characteristic for) angina pectoris and had a normal electrocardiogram. A negative ETT was seen in 23 of 28 patients, and five of 28 patients had a positive ETT. No patients had serial enzyme or electrocardiogram evolution suggestive of myocardial ischemia, and all patients with a negative ETT were discharged after a full inpatient evaluation designed to rule out unstable coronary disease. At a mean follow-up period of 6.1 months there has been no cardiac morbidity or mortality in the patients with negative ETTs. It was concluded that early ETTS of selected ED patients with chest pain is safe, and an exercise test administered during the ED visit which is negative can preclude unnecessary hospitalization.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
